(INTRODUCTION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The year 1914 has no precedent in Stock Exchange history. At the) Tj T*
(present time \(1915\), when the great events that have come to pass are) Tj T*
(still close to us, even their details are vivid in our minds and we) Tj T*
(need no one to rehearse them. Time, however, is quick to dim even) Tj T*
(acute memories, and Wall Street, of all places, is the land of) Tj T*
(forgetfulness. The new happenings of all the World crowd upon each) Tj T*
(other so fast in the financial district that even the greatest and) Tj T*
(most far-reaching of them are soon driven out of sight. This being the) Tj T*
(case, it has seemed to the writer of these pages that some record) Tj T*
(should be kept among the brokerage fraternity of what was so great an) Tj T*
(epoch in their history, and that this record could best be written) Tj T*
(down by one who happened to be very favorably placed to know the story) Tj T*
(in its entirety.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Of course the archives of the Exchange will always contain the minutes) Tj T*
(of Committees and other documentary material embodying the story of) Tj T*
(the past, but this dry chronicle is never likely to see the light) Tj T*
(except when unearthed by law courts or legislative committees. It) Tj T*
(seems worth while, therefore, to disentangle the essential thread of) Tj T*
(the tale of 1914 from the mass of unreadable detail in the minute) Tj T*
(books, and put it in a shape where those who are interested may look) Tj T*
(it over.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
