(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the afternoon of July 30th, the officers of the Stock Exchange met) Tj T*
(in consultation with a number of prominent bankers and bank) Tj T*
(presidents, and the question of closing the Exchange was anxiously) Tj T*
(discussed. While the news from abroad was most critical, and the day's) Tj T*
(decline in prices was alarming, it was also true that no collapse had) Tj T*
(taken place and no money panic had yet appeared. The bankers' opinion) Tj T*
(was unanimous that while closing was a step that might become) Tj T*
(necessary at any time, it was not clear that it would be wise to take) Tj T*
(it that afternoon, and it was agreed to await the events of the) Tj T*
(following day. Meanwhile, several members of the Governing Committee) Tj T*
(of the Exchange had become convinced that closing was inevitable and,) Tj T*
(in opposition to the opinion of the bankers, urged that immediate) Tj T*
(steps be taken to bring it about. It may seem strange to people) Tj T*
(outside of Wall Street that the night before the Exchange closed such) Tj T*
(apparent indecision and difference of opinion existed. It was,) Tj T*
(however, a perfectly natural outcome of an unprecedented situation.) Tj T*
(The crisis had developed so suddenly, and the conditions were so) Tj T*
(utterly without historic parallel, that the best informed men found) Tj T*
(themselves at a loss for guidance.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(During the evening of July 30th the conviction that closing was) Tj T*
(imperative spread with great speed among the large brokerage firms. Up) Tj T*
(to a late hour of the night the President of the Exchange was the) Tj T*
(recipient of many messages and telegrams from houses not only in New) Tj T*
(York, but all over the country, urging immediate action. The paralysis) Tj T*
(of the world's Stock Exchanges had meanwhile become general. The) Tj T*
(Bourses at Montreal, Toronto and Madrid had closed on July 28th; those) Tj T*
(at Vienna, Budapest, Brussels, Antwerp, Berlin, and Rome on July 29th;) Tj T*
(St. Petersburg and all South American countries on July 30th, and on) Tj T*
(this same day the Paris Bourse was likewise forced to suspend) Tj T*
(dealings, first on the Coulisse and then on the Bourse itself. On) Tj T*
(Friday morning, July 31st, the London Stock Exchange officially) Tj T*
(closed, so that the resumption of business on that morning would have) Tj T*
(made New York the only market in which a world panic could vent) Tj T*
(itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Governing Committee of the Exchange were called to meet at nine) Tj T*
(o'clock \(the earliest hour at which they could all be reached, for it) Tj T*
(was summer and many were out of town\) and at that hour they assembled) Tj T*
(in the Secretary's office ready to consider what action should be) Tj T*
(taken. In addition to the Committee many members of prominent firms) Tj T*
(appeared in the room to report that orders to sell stocks at ruinous) Tj T*
(prices were pouring in upon them from all over the world and that) Tj T*
(security holders throughout the country were in a state of panic. It) Tj T*
(would be hopeless to try to describe the nervous tension and) Tj T*
(excitement of the group of perhaps fifty men who consulted together) Tj T*
(under the oppressive consciousness that within forty-five minutes \(it) Tj T*
(was then a quarter past nine\) an unheard of disaster might overtake) Tj T*
(them. It was determined that the Governing Committee should go into) Tj T*
(session at once as there was so little time to spare. Just as they) Tj T*
ET
