(started for their official meeting room a telephone message was) Tj T*
(received from a prominent banking house stating that the bankers and) Tj T*
(bank presidents were holding a consultation and suggesting that the) Tj T*
(Exchange authorities await the conclusion of their deliberations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is an employee of the Exchange whose duty it is to ring a gong) Tj T*
(upon the floor of the big board room at ten o'clock in the morning.) Tj T*
(Until that gong has rung the market is not open and contracts are not) Tj T*
(recognized. This employee was instructed not to ring the gong until he) Tj T*
(had received personal orders to do so from the President; a permanent) Tj T*
(telephone connection was established with the office in which the) Tj T*
(bankers were conferring, and amid a horrible suspense the outcome of) Tj T*
(their conference was awaited. For twenty minutes this strain) Tj T*
(continued. It was a quarter before ten and only fifteen minutes) Tj T*
(remained in which to act. Meanwhile the brokers were fast assembling) Tj T*
(upon the board room floor, orders were piling in upon them to sell at) Tj T*
(panic prices, ten o'clock was approaching, and although all felt that) Tj T*
(the opening should not be permitted no one had a word from the) Tj T*
(Governing Committee as to what was going to be done.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At a quarter of ten, no word having come from the bankers, the) Tj T*
(receiver of the telephone which had been connected with their meeting) Tj T*
(place was hung up, and the Governing Committee were called in session) Tj T*
(to take action. As they took their seats two messages reached them.) Tj T*
(One was brought by a prominent member of their body who had gone to) Tj T*
(the office of the President of the bank Clearing House and had been) Tj T*
(told by him, after consulting with some of his fellow officers, "We) Tj T*
(concur; under no circumstances is it our suggestion, but if the) Tj T*
(Exchange desires to close, we concur." The other was sent, through a) Tj T*
(member of the Exchange, from one of the leading bank Presidents who) Tj T*
(stated that closing would be a grave mistake and that he was opposed) Tj T*
(to it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The roll was called and thirty-six out of the forty-two members) Tj T*
(answered to their names. The Chair having announced the purpose of the) Tj T*
(meeting, Mr. Ernest Groesbeck moved that the Exchange be closed until) Tj T*
(further notice. This motion was carried, not unanimously but by a) Tj T*
